Lab 2: SSH hardening (policy 4.3)

Policy requirement
Administrative access uses SSH only, individual accounts, no direct root login, key-based authentication, and shows a legal warning banner.

Step 1: create a key pair on your admin workstation and copy it to the server

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "admin@sti"
ssh-copy-id student1@192.168.30.10
ssh student1@192.168.30.10 # confirm key login works BEFORE continuing

Step 2: edit the SSH daemon configuration

sudo cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config
PermitRootLogin no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication no
PermitEmptyPasswords no
MaxAuthTries 3
LoginGraceTime 30
AllowUsers student1 netadmin
ClientAliveInterval 300
ClientAliveCountMax 2
X11Forwarding no
Banner /etc/issue.net

Step 3: create the warning banner

sudo nano /etc/issue.net

*** Authorised use only. Activity is monitored and logged. ***
*** Unauthorised access is prohibited and may be prosecuted. ***

Step 4: test the configuration, reload, and verify

sudo sshd -t # no output means the syntax is valid
sudo systemctl reload ssh # service is named sshd on RHEL/CentOS

# from another terminal
ssh root@192.168.30.10 # should be refused
ssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=no student1@192.168.30.10 # should be refused

# show the effective configuration
sudo sshd -T | grep -Ei "permitrootlogin|passwordauthentication|maxauthtries"

Ubuntu includes extra files from /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/. Always check the effective settings with sshd -T because the first value found wins.

